by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-03T07:49:14Z

I reworked the branch along the lines I described above, and rebased it on 8.2. Thanks @Boulea7 for the proposal and @HypeMC for the review, which found most of this.

What changed:

* Wildcards are now opt-in, through `PropertyAccessorBuilder::enableWildcardReads()` or `framework.property_access.wildcard_reads`. With the option off the accessor behaves exactly as before, so both BC breaks are gone: `[*]` still reads the index named `*`, `[\*]` still reads the index named `\*`, and both can still be written to.
* The result shape follows the path instead of the data. One entry per matched element, one nesting level per wildcard, keys dropped. The `array_merge()` flattening is gone, so a fixed path always returns the same shape. It also fixes a case that was not raised yet: a trailing `[*]` used to drop a level, `getValue(['tags' => [['a'], ['b']]], '[tags][*]')` returning `['a', 'b']` instead of `[['a'], ['b']]`.
* `[\*]` now unescapes on the write side too, so it round-trips. The cost, which the description records, is that with the option on an index literally named `\*` can no longer be addressed.
* Writing through a wildcard path throws `InvalidArgumentException`, and `isWritable()` returns `false`. That removes the asymmetry where the setter created a phantom `*` entry that the getter then folded into the result.
* The traversability check is `is_iterable()` instead of `ArrayAccess` and `Traversable` together. The expansion only iterates, and the rest of the path is read from each element, so a plain `Iterator`, an `IteratorAggregate` or a generator works now.

The tests were rewritten around those rules, and two of them pin the behavior with the option off. `PropertyAccess` and `FrameworkBundle` pass, as do `Form`, `Validator` and `Serializer` as consumers.

